course: dictionary
lesson: 6752
title: Onside, on the pitch and in life
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Sport and everyday talk often use the same word in different ways. Onside is one of those words. Read the context to find out which meaning fits. The text gives you the clues. «Simply put, you are offside if, when a teammate passes forward, you are in the other team's half and nearer their goal line than both the ball and the second-last defender; being level is onside.» Read this in pieces, and look for the word that means the opposite of onside.
explain: Offside is the opposite of onside. The clue is «being level is onside»: standing level with the second-last defender still counts as onside, because you are not nearer the goal line than they are. In football, rugby and hockey, onside means you are in a position where you are allowed to play the ball. Try your meaning in the sentence: "you are offside if ... being level is allowed to play the ball." It still makes sense.
explain: Onside has an informal meaning in UK talk too. To get someone onside is to win them over so they agree with you and support you. Read the words around it: a person who is onside with your plan is on your side. The same word, two different jobs. Context tells you which one you need.
fact: «Onside» means in a position where you are allowed to play the ball.
fact: «Offside» is the opposite of onside.
fact: Being level with the second-last defender counts as onside.
fact: To get someone onside is to win them over so they agree with you and support you. This is an informal, everyday use.
fact: Onside can be an adjective («an onside player») or an adverb («she stayed onside»).
exercise: Coach shouts, «Stay onside!» What does she mean? | Shout at the referee | *Stay in a position where you are allowed to play the ball | Run as fast as you can to the goal | Stand still and clap
exercise: Jay says onside means being ahead of everyone near the goal. Is he right? | *No, onside means you are allowed to play the ball | No, it means you are asleep | Yes, the goal line is always ahead | Yes, you are always nearer the goal
exercise: Ella wants to get the whole class onside before the class vote. What does get onside mean here? | *Win them over so they agree with her and support her | Tell them to be quiet | Send them to the back of the line | Make them run onto the field
test: What is the opposite of onside? | *Offside | Outside | Inside | Level
test: The text says being level with the second-last defender is onside. Which is true? | *Standing level with the second-last defender is onside | Standing level is always offside | Level means you cannot play the ball | Being level means you are in the goal
exam: In «Get the captain onside before the match», what does onside mean? | Standing in the goal | Running very fast | Wearing a bib | *Agreeing with you and supporting you
